    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d741">

                <pb facs="0710" n="698" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg698"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A Recruiting/31: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador to Poland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Biddle</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">Then in France, to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-10-07T14:00:00+01:00">October 7, 1939—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 7—11:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>3. 1. Your number 1136, October 2, 1939, 9 p.m. Minister Zaleski<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d741-f1"/> expressed his deep regret that General Sikorsky’s
                    statement had created an unfavorable impression in the United States. Zaleski
                    assured me (<hi rendition="#italic">a</hi>) he was familiar with the provisions
                    of the President’s proclamation of neutrality relating to the enlistment of our
                    soldiers and marines and (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) that when General
                    Sikorsky made aforementioned statement he had in mind only those Poles who might
                    not yet have acquired full American citizenship, a status similar to that of
                    many Poles now in France.</p>
                <p>2. I am aware moreover that since date of Sikorsky’s aforementioned statement the
                    new Polish Government has assumed more definite form a fact which will
                    henceforth afford Government officials the benefit of consultation on all
                    aspects of envisaged public utterances previous to their issuance.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d741-f1" n="59" xml:id="d741-f1-content">
                <p>August Zaleski, Polish Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
